State is data that belongs to a React component and can change over time.
When state changes, React re-renders the component and shows the updated value.

syntax :- const [state, setState] = useState(initialValue);
state → State Value
setState → Setter Function
initialValue → Initial/Starting Value

so 'state' and 'setState' are not fixed names.
choose the different names for examples given below :-
const [name, setName] = useState("Arun");
const [age, setAge] = useState(25);
const [isLoggedIn, setIsLoggedIn] = useState(false);

The main topics :-

● Local State
Local state means state that is used inside one component.
We normally create local state using useState().

● Updating State
Update State using the Setter Function.

● State Batching
Batching means React groups multiple state updates together and performs one re-render instead of re-rendering after every update.

Previous State (prevState) :-
--------------------------
whenever new state depends on old state, use: setState(prevState => prevState + newValueBasedOn);
setState → updates the state
prevState → previous state value
=> → function
prevState + newValueBasedOn → new value calculated from the previous state

This is the main idea behind "Updating State Based on Previous State.

it works for a simple single update ➔ setState(state + 1);
when the new state depends on the previous state, React recommends the functional form ➔ setState(prevState => prevState + 1); 
React, take the latest state value, add 1 to it, and make that the new state ➔ setState(prevState => prevState + 1);

Functional Updater: prevState => prevState + 1
Setter Function with Functional Updater: setState(prevState => prevState + 1);

It is especially important when you have multiple state updates or React batches updates.

Example :-
const increase = () => {
  setCount(prev => prev + 1);
  setCount(prev => prev + 1);
  setCount(prev => prev + 1);
};

React processes them using the latest previous value :-
Starting Count = 0
First Update: prev = 0 → 1
Second Update: prev = 1 → 2
Third Update: prev = 2 → 3

Result :-
Count: 3
